Q: What are drawbacks or disadvantages of the singleton pattern? A: <p>One rather bad thing about singletons is that you can't extend them very easily. You basically have to build in some kind of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decorator_pattern" rel="nofollow noreferrer">decorator pattern</a> or some such thing if you want to change their behavior. Also, if one day you want to have multiple ways of doing that one thing, it can be rather painful to change, depending on how you lay out your code.</p>
<p>One thing to note, if you <em>do</em> use singletons, try to pass them in to whoever needs them rather than have them access it directly... Otherwise, if you ever choose to have multiple ways of doing the thing that singleton does, it will be rather difficult to change as each class embeds a dependency if it accesses the singleton directly.</p>
<p>So basically:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public MyConstructor(Singleton singleton) {
    this.singleton = singleton;
}
</code></pre>
<p>rather than:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public MyConstructor() {
    this.singleton = Singleton.getInstance();
}
</code></pre>
<p>I believe this sort of pattern is called <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dependency_injection" rel="nofollow noreferrer">dependency injection</a> and is generally considered a good thing.</p>
<p>Like any pattern though... Think about it and consider if its use in the given situation is inappropriate or not... Rules are made to be broken usually, and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Software_design_pattern" rel="nofollow noreferrer">patterns</a> should not be applied willy-nilly without thought.</p>

Q: Adding Multiple Guests to Google Calendar Event via Apps Script and Google Calendar API A: <p>The attendees property needs to be an array of objects, but the tricky part is that you have to map both the <code>email</code> and <code>displayName</code> in parallel. Something like this should work:</p>
<pre><code>attendees: emails.map((email, index) =&gt; (
    {
        email: email,
        displayName: names[index]
    }
)),
</code></pre>
<p>Also, you should also add a check that the emails and names arrays have the same <code>length</code> and decide how to handle if they aren't.</p>
<p>Also also, you can simplify the loop to be <code>for (const shift of events)</code> and the fields inside the loop can be destructured like this:</p>
<pre><code>    const [eventsubject, startTime, location, description, color, status, attendeesNames, attendeesEmail] = shift;
</code></pre>
<p>Also also also, how are Pickles and Pancakes doing?</p>

Q: How to insert an SVG character in Mathjax for my website A: <p>The post that @herrstrietzel references tells you several ways to insert an SVG image into a MathJax expression.  It doesn't talk about putting sub-expressions under the image because once the image is there, you just need to use the regular LaTeX macros that handle that sort of thing.  For example, if you want an upright <code>X</code> to act like a summation symbol (where the lists will go underneath it in display style, but act as super- and subscripts in text style), then you would use <code>$$\mathop{\mathrm{X}}_{n=1}^{10}$$</code> to get</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/82N4UdfT.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/82N4UdfT.png" alt="X with limits above and below" /></a></p>
<p>Those are just normal LaTeX commands, so not related to whether they apply to a character or an image.  That is why @herrstrietzel is correct to refer you to that answer, as the image is the only thing you need to handle specially; the placement of limits is standard LaTeX.</p>
<p>With that in mind, you can combine the answer he linked to with things like <code>\mathop</code> to achieve what you are trying to do.  I would recommend using the <code>\mmlToken</code> approach, as that is straight-forward.  Note that the bounding box for the <code>mglyph</code> that it creates will be the bounding box of the SVG image as given in its <code>viewBox</code> attribute.</p>
<p>The image in your post, however, has some problems with its <code>viewBox</code>.  There is excessive height and depth to the box (extra space above and below), and insufficient width (the image is clipped), as can be seen here where I have added a red background to make the extent of the image visible:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/wig5BjaY.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/wig5BjaY.png" alt="SVG with bad bounding box shown by red background" /></a></p>
<p>Using the SVG as it is would cause the limits to be too far away from the ink of the character, and the symbol would be clipped on the left and right.</p>
<p>Adjusting the view box to be <code>-10 35 173 216</code> produces a better result:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/TQxczZJj.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/TQxczZJj.png" alt="SVG with better bounding box" /></a></p>
<p>Using that SVG would produce layouts where the spacing is appropriate.  Here is a live example:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@4/tex-chtml.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;div style="font-size: 120%"&gt;
$$\mathop{\mmlToken{mglyph}[src="data:image/svg+xml;base64," width="17.3px" height="21.6px" valign="-4px"]{}}_{x\to u}f(x)$$
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Here I have used an <a href="https://svgview.com/svg-to-data-uri" rel="nofollow noreferrer">SVG to Data URL converter</a> to produce the <code>data:</code> URL used in the <code>\mmlToken</code> call.  You could use that URL as well, or could link to an <code>.svg</code> file directly.  You will need to adjust the <code>height</code>, <code>width</code>, and <code>valign</code> attributes based on the font size you are using.  I used a size that would make these easy to compute from the <code>viewBox</code>.</p>
<p>Note that this approach will not change the size automatically for display style versus text style (or the two script styles), as is usually the case with large operators in LaTeX.  If you want to make that happen, you can use the <code>\mathchoice</code> macro to select a different size automatically based on the current style.  Here is one approach:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@4/tex-chtml.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;span style="display:none"&gt;
\(\def\ACOP#1#2#3{\mmlToken{mglyph}[src="data:image/svg+xml;base64," width="#1px" height="#2px" valign="#3px"]{}}
\def\dAcop{\ACOP{24.3}{30.5}{-8}}
\def\tAcop{\ACOP{17.3}{21.6}{-4}}
\def\sAcop{\ACOP{12.2}{15.3}{-2.8}}
\def\ssAcop{\ACOP{8.7}{10.8}{-2}}
\def\Acop{\mathop{\mathchoice{\dAcop}{\tAcop}{\sAcop}{\ssAcop}}_}\)
&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;div style="font-size: 120%"&gt;
$${\displaystyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}+{\textstyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}+{\scriptstyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}+{\scriptscriptstyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}$$
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Here I define a macro <code>\ACOP</code> that takes three arguments (the width, height, and valign values) and produces the <code>mglyph</code> with the proper attributes.  Then I define four macros, one for each typesetting style.  Finally, I define <code>\Acop</code> to use <code>\mathchoice</code> to pick the needed one, and wrap it all in a <code>\mathop</code> so that it handles the limits properly.  Since your image suggested you wanted to be able to do <code>\Acop{x\to u}f(x)</code>, I have included the <code>_</code> in the macro definition so that it will take the next brace group as the lower limit.  All the definitions are made within a hidden <code>span</code> element, so they don't take up space in the document (but see below for another way to do that).</p>
<p>If you don't want the display-style size to be larger, just use <code>\tAcop</code> in place if <code>\dAcop</code> in the <code>\mathchoice</code> call.</p>
<p>Note that the limits are placed as subscripts in all styles other than display style.  This is the standard for how operators work (to prevent disrupting the inter-line spacing within a paragraph).  If you always want the limits to appear below, then use <code>\limits</code> before the <code>_</code>:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@4/tex-chtml.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;span style="display:none"&gt;
\(\def\ACOP#1#2#3{\mmlToken{mglyph}[src="data:image/svg+xml;base64," width="#1px" height="#2px" valign="#3px"]{}}
\def\dAcop{\ACOP{24.3}{30.5}{-8}}
\def\tAcop{\ACOP{17.3}{21.6}{-4}}
\def\sAcop{\ACOP{12.2}{15.3}{-2.8}}
\def\ssAcop{\ACOP{8.7}{10.8}{-2}}
\def\Acop{\mathop{\mathchoice{\dAcop}{\tAcop}{\sAcop}{\ssAcop}}\limits_}\)
&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;div style="font-size: 120%"&gt;
$${\displaystyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}+{\textstyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}+{\scriptstyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}+{\scriptscriptstyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}$$
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Note that when the limits are below the symbol, the width of the result is the larger of the widths of the SVG and of the limits below it, so in the example above, the text style version has the &quot;f&quot; starting just to the right of the &quot;u&quot;, so there is space between the &quot;h&quot; SVG and the &quot;f&quot;.  If you like less space, you can use <code>\!</code> to reduce the space, as in <code>\Acop{x\to u}\!\!f(x)</code>.</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/7oFXX72e.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/7oFXX72e.png" alt="SVG with reduced space before f(x)" /></a></p>
<p>Since the amount of backspacing is dependent on the actual limits below (and the typesetting style), the space can't be part of the macro, and must be inserted by hand. Alternatively, you could load the <code>mathtools</code> package and use its <code>\matclap{}</code> macro to hide the size of the limit (though if you used very wide limits, that could be problematic).  For example <code>\Acop{\mathclap{x\to u}}f(x)</code> produces</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/pvWVP1fg.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/pvWVP1fg.png" alt="Using \mathclap around the limits" /></a></p>
<p>Note that this removes the space on the left as well as the right, which might not be appropriate.  It would be possible to include <code>\mathclap</code> into the definition of <code>\Acap</code> using</p>
<pre class="lang-latex prettyprint-override"><code>\def\Acop#1{\mathop{\mathchoice{\dAcop}{\tAcop}{\sAcop}{\ssAcop}}\limits_{\mathclap{#1}}
</code></pre>
<p>though that would make it difficult to use limits that are wide.</p>
<p>Finally, I mentioned that there is another way to define the needed macros.  If you are managing your own pages, you can define the macros in the MathJax configuration object:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@4/tex-chtml.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;script&gt;
MathJax = {
  tex: {
    macros: {
      ACOP: ['\\mmlToken{mglyph}[src="data:image/svg+xml;base64," width="#1px" height="#2px" valign="#3px"]{}', 3],
      dAcop: '\\ACOP{24.3}{30.5}{-8}',
      tAcop: '\\ACOP{17.3}{21.6}{-4}',
      sAcop: '\\ACOP{12.2}{15.3}{-2.8}',
      ssAcop: '\\ACOP{8.7}{10.8}{-2}',
      Acop: '\\mathop{\\mathchoice{\\dAcop}{\\tAcop}{\\sAcop}{\\ssAcop}}\\limits_',
    }
  }
};
&lt;/script&gt;

$${\displaystyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}+{\textstyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}+{\scriptstyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}+{\scriptscriptstyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}$$</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>If you want to include <code>\mathclap</code> in the <code>\Acop</code> definition, then use</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@4/tex-chtml.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;script&gt;
MathJax = {
  loader: {load: ['[tex]/mathtools']},
  tex: {
    packages: {'[+]': ['mathtools']},
    macros: {
      ACOP: ['\\mmlToken{mglyph}[src="data:image/svg+xml;base64," width="#1px" height="#2px" valign="#3px"]{}', 3],
      dAcop: '\\ACOP{24.3}{30.5}{-8}',
      tAcop: '\\ACOP{17.3}{21.6}{-4}',
      sAcop: '\\ACOP{12.2}{15.3}{-2.8}',
      ssAcop: '\\ACOP{8.7}{10.8}{-2}',
      Acop: ['\\mathop{\\mathchoice{\\dAcop}{\\tAcop}{\\sAcop}{\\ssAcop}}\\limits_{\\mathclap{#1}}', 1],
    }
  }
};
&lt;/script&gt;

$${\displaystyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}+{\textstyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}+{\scriptstyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}+{\scriptscriptstyle\Acop{x\to u}f(x)}$$</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>I think that should cover all the bases for you.</p>
